-- certifications_cloud_architecture.sql
--
-- Why: the founder ruled that every position must hold the top certifications for its role and
-- that every agent must sit and pass them; "if they dont pass them all, then their knowledge is
-- simply not enough". This file authors the four cloud-and-architecture question banks that the
-- product_eng and data_intel seats sit: AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional,
-- AWS Certified Developer - Associate, TOGAF 9 Certified and EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional.
-- 40 questions each, at real exam difficulty (scenario and application, never definitions),
-- four options, one key, a two-sentence rationale, the syllabus area named as the issuing body
-- names it, 10 of 40 marked 'hard' and the key letter spread exactly ten times over A/B/C/D.
-- These seats run a Postgres-first serverless estate, so transactions and isolation levels,
-- indexing and query plans, connection pooling, row level security, triggers, partial and
-- expression indexes, backup and point in time recovery and event driven design are weighted
-- heavily in the Postgres and AWS banks.
--
-- The role_certification rows and the sitter are authored by a sibling file; this file writes
-- question banks only. Each bank is guarded so that the first author of a shared certification
-- wins and a re-apply is a no-op.
--
-- Apply: node scripts/apply_sql.mjs scripts/sql/certifications_cloud_architecture.sql --apply --ddl

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design Solutions for Organizational Complexity','exam','A company runs 200 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations. Security requires that no member account can disable AWS CloudTrail, including through the account root user. What achieves this with the least ongoing effort?','Attach an IAM policy denying cloudtrail:StopLogging to every IAM user in each account','Enable an AWS Config rule that re-enables the trail whenever it is stopped','Attach a service control policy at the organization root denying cloudtrail:StopLogging and cloudtrail:DeleteTrail','Create a CloudWatch Events rule in each account that alerts the security team','C','A service control policy bounds the maximum permissions of every principal in a member account, including the root user, which an in-account IAM policy cannot do. One SCP at the organization root covers all 200 accounts with no per-account work.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design Solutions for Organizational Complexity','exam','An enterprise wants engineers who authenticate through its existing SAML identity provider to assume roles in 40 member accounts, with permissions varying by team. Which design is best?','Federate through IAM Identity Center with permission sets assigned to groups and accounts','Create IAM users in each of the 40 accounts and rotate their access keys weekly','Create one shared IAM role per account with an inline policy allowing all actions','Issue long lived access keys from a central account and distribute them through Secrets Manager','A','IAM Identity Center federates the existing SAML provider once and projects permission sets into every member account as short lived role sessions. The other options create long lived credentials or discard least privilege.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design Solutions for Organizational Complexity','hard','Traffic between 30 spoke VPCs must be inspected by a central firewall appliance and spokes must not reach each other directly. Which AWS Transit Gateway configuration is correct?','One route table with every attachment associated and propagated','Appliance mode on the spoke attachments with a single default route table','Peer every spoke VPC with the inspection VPC and stop using the transit gateway','Two route tables: a spoke table sending 0.0.0.0/0 to the inspection attachment, and an inspection table with the spoke CIDRs propagated, with appliance mode enabled on the inspection VPC attachment','D','Separate route tables stop spoke to spoke traffic and force everything through the inspection attachment, and appliance mode on the inspection attachment keeps both directions of a flow on the same appliance AZ. A single shared route table would let spokes route directly to each other.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design Solutions for Organizational Complexity','exam','A management account wants Savings Plans discounts shared across member accounts but must exclude one account that holds a regulated workload billed separately. What is correct?','Purchase Savings Plans separately inside each member account','Leave discount sharing on at the organization level and turn sharing off for that single account in the billing preferences','Move the regulated account out of the organization','Buy only On-Demand Capacity Reservations for that account','B','Billing preferences allow discount and Savings Plans sharing to be disabled per account while the rest of the organization keeps sharing. Removing the account from the organization would lose consolidated billing and every guardrail with it.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design for New Solutions','exam','A payments service must process events for a given customer strictly in order, absorb bursts, and scale to thousands of customers. Which design fits?','An SNS standard topic fanning out to Lambda','An SQS FIFO queue using the customer id as the message group id, consumed by Lambda','A Kinesis data stream with a random partition key','An SQS standard queue with a one hour visibility timeout','B','FIFO queues guarantee ordering within a message group, so a per customer group id preserves order per customer while allowing many groups to be processed in parallel. A random Kinesis partition key destroys per customer ordering.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design for New Solutions','exam','A relational workload needs an RPO measured in seconds and an RTO under one minute across two Regions. Which option meets it?','Automated RDS snapshots copied to the second Region every hour','A cross Region read replica promoted manually after an operator confirms the outage','A nightly logical dump restored into a standby cluster','An Aurora global database with a secondary Region cluster and managed failover','D','Aurora global database replicates with typical lag around one second and supports managed planned and unplanned failover in well under a minute. Hourly snapshots give an hourly RPO and manual promotion cannot hold a one minute RTO.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design for New Solutions','hard','A Lambda backed API opens a new PostgreSQL connection per invocation and exhausts max_connections during traffic spikes. Which change fixes the root cause with the least code?','Put RDS Proxy in front of the database and point the function at the proxy endpoint','Raise max_connections on the instance to 10000','Set provisioned concurrency to 1 for the function','Move the connection open call inside the handler body','A','RDS Proxy multiplexes many short lived client connections onto a small pool of database connections, which is exactly the mismatch a per invocation connection creates. Raising max_connections multiplies per backend memory and context switching instead of removing the churn.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design for New Solutions','exam','A multi tenant SaaS keeps every tenant in one PostgreSQL schema and must guarantee that an application bug cannot return another tenant rows. Which control is strongest?','Add a tenant id predicate to every application query and enforce it in code review','Give each tenant its own database user but leave the tables readable by public','Enable row level security with a policy comparing the tenant id column to a per connection setting, and set that setting when the connection is checked out','Encrypt the tenant id column with a KMS key','C','Row level security is enforced by the database on every statement, so a missing predicate in application code cannot leak rows. Code review and encryption of the discriminator column do not stop a query that simply omits the filter.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design for New Solutions','exam','An analytics team queries 40 TB of raw JSON in Amazon S3 with Athena and complains about both cost and latency. Which change improves both the most?','Convert the data to partitioned, compressed Parquet','Move the data to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval','Increase the Athena query timeout','Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket','A','Athena bills on bytes scanned, and columnar Parquet with partition pruning cuts the scanned volume by an order of magnitude while also reducing runtime. Storage class and transfer acceleration change neither the bytes a query must read nor the parse cost.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design for New Solutions','exam','On premises servers must resolve names in a Route 53 private hosted zone, and instances in the VPC must resolve on premises names. What is required?','A public hosted zone carrying the same records','A Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoint only','A Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoint for queries arriving from on premises, plus an outbound endpoint with forwarding rules for the on premises zones','DNS forwarding configured on the internet gateway','C','Resolution is directional: the inbound endpoint gives on premises resolvers a target inside the VPC, and the outbound endpoint with rules forwards VPC queries for on premises zones to the corporate resolvers. One endpoint alone only solves one direction.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Continuous Improvement for Existing Solutions','hard','A reporting job on Aurora PostgreSQL runs many statements in one transaction and sometimes sees a row inserted after the job started, which breaks its totals. What is the correct fix?','Run each statement in autocommit under read committed','Begin the job transaction with repeatable read isolation so every statement sees one snapshot','Take an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock on every table for the duration','Set the transaction isolation level to read uncommitted','B','Under read committed each statement takes a fresh snapshot, so a long multi statement job sees concurrent commits; repeatable read fixes one snapshot for the whole transaction. PostgreSQL treats read uncommitted as read committed, so it changes nothing.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Continuous Improvement for Existing Solutions','exam','A team cannot tell which of 20 microservices behind API Gateway causes p99 latency. Which change gives the most direct answer?','Raise CloudWatch metric resolution to one second','Enable VPC flow logs on every subnet','Ship all application logs into a single log group','Enable AWS X-Ray tracing end to end and read the service map and trace segments','D','Distributed tracing attributes latency to individual downstream segments across service boundaries, which aggregate metrics and logs cannot do. Flow logs record packet metadata and say nothing about application latency.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Continuous Improvement for Existing Solutions','exam','Objects in a bucket have unpredictable access patterns and range from 1 KB to 5 GB. The team wants automatic savings with no retrieval risk. What should they do?','Lifecycle transition everything to Glacier Deep Archive after 30 days','Enable Requester Pays on the bucket','Store everything in One Zone Infrequent Access','Enable S3 Intelligent-Tiering, accepting that objects under 128 KB are always billed at the frequent access tier','D','Intelligent-Tiering moves objects between access tiers on observed usage with no retrieval fee, which suits unpredictable patterns. Deep Archive introduces hours of retrieval latency and One Zone accepts a durability trade off the question does not allow.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Continuous Improvement for Existing Solutions','exam','A team wants an Amazon ECS release where a bad version reaches only a small share of traffic and rolls back automatically on an alarm. What should they use?','A rolling update with minimum healthy percent set to 100','CodeDeploy blue green deployment with canary traffic shifting and CloudWatch alarms configured for automatic rollback','Registering a new task definition and restarting the service','A second cluster switched over by editing DNS manually','B','CodeDeploy blue green shifts a defined percentage to the replacement task set and reverts on an alarm without redeploying. Rolling updates replace tasks in place with no traffic percentage control and no automated revert.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Continuous Improvement for Existing Solutions','hard','A query filtering on a status column equal to open together with a creation timestamp in the last seven days scans a 400 million row table, although only 0.3 percent of rows are open. Which index serves it best?','A hash index on the status column','A btree index on the timestamp column alone','A partial btree index on the timestamp column with a WHERE clause restricting it to rows whose status is open','A GIN index over the whole row','C','A partial index stores only the 0.3 percent of qualifying rows and is ordered by the timestamp, so it is small enough to stay cached and satisfies both predicates. A hash index supports only equality and cannot serve the range, and an index on the timestamp alone still reads mostly non-open rows.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Continuous Improvement for Existing Solutions','exam','A read heavy product API repeatedly reads the same 500 items from the database. Which change gives the largest latency and cost improvement with the least rewriting?','Put ElastiCache in front of the database with a read through pattern and a short TTL','Increase the RDS instance size by two steps','Add three read replicas and round robin between them','Enable Multi-AZ on the primary instance','A','An in memory cache in front of a small, hot working set removes almost all database round trips at a fraction of the cost of larger instances. Multi-AZ is a availability feature and adds no read capacity at all.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Accelerate Workload Migration and Modernization','exam','A legacy Windows application must reach AWS in eight weeks with no code change and minimal risk. Which of the seven Rs applies?','Refactor onto containers running on Amazon ECS','Repurchase as a SaaS product','Rehost using AWS Application Migration Service','Retire the application','C','Rehosting replicates the server as it is and cuts over, which is the fastest low risk path when code cannot change. Refactoring and repurchasing both require change the timeline does not allow.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Accelerate Workload Migration and Modernization','exam','600 TB must reach Amazon S3 from a site with a 200 Mbps uplink within three weeks. What is appropriate?','AWS DataSync over the existing link','S3 multipart upload with Transfer Acceleration','A newly ordered 1 Gbps Direct Connect circuit','AWS Snow Family devices shipped, loaded and imported into S3','D','At 200 Mbps a 600 TB transfer needs roughly nine months even at full line rate, so no online method fits the window. Physical devices move the data in days, and a new Direct Connect circuit takes weeks to provision before the transfer even starts.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Accelerate Workload Migration and Modernization','hard','An Oracle database must move to Aurora PostgreSQL with under 15 minutes of downtime. Which sequence is correct?','Convert the schema with AWS Schema Conversion Tool, run an AWS DMS task with full load plus change data capture, then cut over when replication lag is near zero','Take an Oracle RMAN backup and restore it into Aurora','Run a DMS full load only, stopping the source for the duration of the load','Export CSV files to S3 and reload them nightly until the teams agree to switch','A','Heterogeneous migration needs schema conversion first, and full load plus ongoing CDC keeps the target current so the outage is only the cutover. A full load only task requires the source to be quiesced for the whole copy, and RMAN backups are not restorable into PostgreSQL.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Accelerate Workload Migration and Modernization','exam','A monolith must be decomposed without a big bang cutover. Which pattern routes a subset of endpoints to new services while the monolith serves the rest?','Blue green deployment of the entire monolith','A strangler fig facade at the API layer routing selected paths to new services and everything else to the monolith','Lift and shift onto larger instances','A read replica of the monolith database exposed directly to clients','B','The strangler fig pattern puts a routing facade in front so functionality migrates path by path and can be reverted per path. Blue green still swaps the whole application at once.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design Solutions for Organizational Complexity','exam','A network account owns subnets that application accounts must launch resources into. Which service enables this?','VPC peering between every pair of accounts','AWS Resource Access Manager sharing the subnets with the organization','AWS PrivateLink endpoint services','Recreating the same CIDR block in each account','B','Resource Access Manager shares subnets so participant accounts launch into the owner VPC, keeping one address space and one set of route tables. Peering connects separate VPCs and does not let another account launch into your subnet.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design Solutions for Organizational Complexity','exam','A company wants every new account created with baseline logging, guardrails and a repeatable provisioning process. Which is the least effort?','AWS Control Tower with its landing zone and Account Factory','A CloudFormation StackSet run by hand for each new account','A Lambda that copies settings after an engineer files a ticket','A shared VPC and nothing else','A','Control Tower provisions the landing zone, the log archive and audit accounts, and applies preventive and detective controls to every account the Account Factory creates. The alternatives all leave the baseline to a manual step that will eventually be skipped.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design for New Solutions','hard','An order table must publish an event to downstream services when a row reaches the shipped status, and the event must never be published if the transaction rolls back. What is correct?','An AFTER UPDATE trigger that calls an HTTP endpoint through an extension','A BEFORE UPDATE trigger that writes to a foreign table','A LISTEN and NOTIFY payload consumed by a worker with no persistence','A transactional outbox row written by the same transaction, polled and published by a separate relay process','D','Writing the event to an outbox table inside the business transaction makes publication atomic with the state change, and the relay retries safely because the row survives a crash. Calling out over HTTP or to a foreign table from a trigger performs an external side effect that a rollback cannot undo.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design for New Solutions','exam','A Lambda function behind API Gateway must return a 25 MB PDF to browsers. What is the correct design?','Increase the Lambda memory to 10240 MB','Return the file base64 encoded in the API response','Write the file to S3 and return a presigned URL, because the API Gateway response payload limit is 10 MB','Stream the file through a Network Load Balancer','C','API Gateway caps the response payload at 10 MB and Lambda at 6 MB, so a 25 MB body cannot be returned inline whatever the memory setting. A presigned URL hands the download directly to S3 with no size problem.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Accelerate Workload Migration and Modernization','exam','A security team wants continuous detection of compromised EC2 credentials and cryptomining behaviour across every account, with no agents to install. Which service should be enabled organization wide?','Amazon GuardDuty with a delegated administrator for the organization','AWS Config conformance packs','Amazon Inspector for CVE scanning','AWS Trusted Advisor','A','GuardDuty analyses CloudTrail, VPC flow logs and DNS logs to detect credential misuse and mining behaviour without deploying anything on the instances. Config evaluates configuration state and Inspector scans for vulnerabilities, neither of which detects live misuse.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design Solutions for Organizational Complexity','exam','A regulator requires that the company can cryptographically deny AWS access to its data and control the key material itself. Which meets it?','An AWS managed KMS key','S3 server side encryption with S3 managed keys','A customer managed KMS key using AWS generated key material','AWS CloudHSM backed keys, or a KMS key with imported key material the company can delete','D','Only imported key material or a CloudHSM custom key store leaves the company able to destroy the material and render the data unreadable. Keys generated and held by KMS do not give the customer that unilateral control.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Continuous Improvement for Existing Solutions','hard','After a bulk load of 50 million rows a query that previously used an index now scans sequentially, and the plan estimates one row where millions are returned. What is the first correct action?','REINDEX CONCURRENTLY every index on the table','Raise work_mem to 4 GB','Run ANALYZE on the table so the planner has current statistics','Set enable_seqscan to off permanently','C','A wildly low row estimate after a bulk load is the signature of stale statistics, and ANALYZE restores the planner inputs. Disabling sequential scans hides the symptom globally and will damage other queries.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Accelerate Workload Migration and Modernization','exam','A team must run Linux containers with no EC2 capacity to manage, per task IAM roles, and no kernel shared between tasks. What should they choose?','The ECS EC2 launch type on a shared cluster','AWS Fargate tasks with task roles','Lambda container images','Elastic Beanstalk single container Docker','B','Fargate runs each task in its own isolated microVM with no instances to patch and supports task level IAM roles. The EC2 launch type shares a host kernel between tasks and leaves capacity management to the team.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Accelerate Workload Migration and Modernization','exam','Before migrating 900 servers the team needs dependency mapping and right sizing recommendations. Which tool is appropriate?','AWS Pricing Calculator','AWS Application Migration Service','AWS Config','AWS Application Discovery Service feeding AWS Migration Hub','D','Application Discovery Service collects utilisation and network dependency data from the existing estate and Migration Hub aggregates it into recommendations and wave planning. Application Migration Service performs the replication after that assessment is done.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design Solutions for Organizational Complexity','exam','Account B must read objects in an S3 bucket in account A that are encrypted with a customer managed KMS key in account A. What must be configured?','Only the bucket policy in account A','Only an IAM policy in account B','The KMS key policy in account A allowing account B, an IAM policy in account B allowing the KMS and S3 actions, and a bucket policy allowing the principal','Nothing, because KMS keys cannot be used across accounts','C','Cross account access to an encrypted object needs three aligned grants: the resource policy on the bucket, the key policy on the KMS key, and the identity policy in the calling account. Any one of them missing produces AccessDenied.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design Solutions for Organizational Complexity','hard','A role in a member account has an identity policy allowing all S3 actions, an SCP that allows only s3:GetObject, and a permissions boundary that allows only s3:PutObject. What can the role do on S3?','GetObject and PutObject','Nothing, because the effective permission is the intersection of all three and that intersection is empty','PutObject only','GetObject only','B','An action is allowed only if the identity policy, the permissions boundary and every applicable SCP all allow it. Here the boundary and the SCP have no action in common, so the intersection is empty.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design for New Solutions','exam','A global TCP game service needs static anycast IP addresses and fast failover between Regions. Which service fits?','AWS Global Accelerator','Amazon CloudFront','Route 53 latency records in front of a Network Load Balancer per Region','An Application Load Balancer spanning Regions','A','Global Accelerator provides two static anycast IPs, routes over the AWS backbone and fails over between regional endpoints in seconds without DNS involvement. Route 53 failover waits for client DNS caches to expire and CloudFront is oriented to HTTP content delivery.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design for New Solutions','exam','A pipeline ingests 1 GB per second of telemetry, and several independent consumers must be able to replay the last 24 hours. Which service is the best fit?','Amazon SQS standard queues','Amazon SNS with SQS subscribers','Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with 24 hour retention and enhanced fan-out consumers','Amazon MQ with a topic per consumer','C','Kinesis retains the stream for a configured window so multiple consumers read independently at their own position and can replay. Queues delete a message once it is consumed, which makes independent replay impossible.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Continuous Improvement for Existing Solutions','exam','An application fails whenever one Availability Zone degrades, even though instances exist elsewhere behind an Application Load Balancer. What is the most likely cause?','Cross zone load balancing is enabled','The target group has healthy targets in only one AZ, and the subnets in the other AZs are not associated with the load balancer','The instances have no public IP addresses','Sticky sessions are enabled','B','An ALB only sends traffic to Availability Zones whose subnets are associated with it and which contain healthy targets. If capacity is effectively single AZ, the load balancer has nowhere else to send requests.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Continuous Improvement for Existing Solutions','hard','A 3 TB events table keeps 90 days of history, and a nightly job deletes older rows, causing bloat and very long vacuums. What is the right change?','Range partition the table by day and drop whole partitions instead of deleting rows','Run VACUUM FULL nightly during the delete window','Increase autovacuum_vacuum_cost_delay','Add a btree index on the timestamp and delete in a single statement','A','Dropping a partition is a catalog operation that reclaims the space immediately and creates no dead tuples for vacuum to chase. Bulk DELETE leaves 30 days of dead rows per cycle, and VACUUM FULL takes an exclusive lock and rewrites the whole table.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Accelerate Workload Migration and Modernization','exam','An enterprise must run Windows Server under existing licences that require visibility of physical sockets and cores. Which option satisfies the licensing terms?','Shared tenancy EC2 with licence included AMIs','Windows containers on Fargate','Dedicated Instances','EC2 Dedicated Hosts with host affinity, tracked in AWS License Manager','D','Dedicated Hosts expose the physical socket and core counts and support host affinity, which is what socket or core based bring your own licence terms require. Dedicated Instances give hardware isolation but not the physical host visibility.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Accelerate Workload Migration and Modernization','exam','A steady compute baseline runs on Fargate, Lambda and several EC2 instance families. Which commitment gives the broadest coverage?','Compute Savings Plans','Standard Reserved Instances for one instance family','EC2 Instance Savings Plans','Spot Instances only','A','Compute Savings Plans apply automatically across EC2 of any family and Region, Fargate and Lambda, which matches a mixed baseline. EC2 Instance Savings Plans and Reserved Instances are locked to a family and Region and cover neither Fargate nor Lambda.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design Solutions for Organizational Complexity','exam','Audit requires immutable central storage of CloudTrail logs from every account, readable by security, with no member account able to delete them. Which design is correct?','A CloudWatch log group in each account with ten year retention','An organization trail delivering to an S3 bucket in a dedicated log archive account with S3 Object Lock in compliance mode','Each account writing to its own bucket with versioning enabled','A nightly copy job run by an operator','B','An organization trail is configured once in the management account and cannot be turned off by members, and Object Lock in compliance mode prevents deletion by anyone including the bucket owner. Per account buckets leave deletion in the hands of the account being audited.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Design for New Solutions','hard','A Lambda consumer of an SQS standard queue occasionally charges a customer twice. Which change is the correct fix?','Set the queue visibility timeout equal to the function timeout','Enable long polling on the queue','Set the maximum receive count to one so a message is never retried','Make the charge idempotent by keying on a client supplied idempotency token stored with a unique constraint, so a repeat delivery is a no-op','D','Standard queues guarantee at least once delivery, so any consumer must be idempotent rather than assume exactly once. Removing retries would silently drop payments on a transient failure instead of preventing the duplicate.'),
('AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional','Continuous Improvement for Existing Solutions','exam','An Auto Scaling group scales on average CPU, but the work backlog grows during spikes because instances take minutes to warm up. Which improvement is most effective?','Increase the cooldown period','Move to a fixed capacity sized for peak','Use a target tracking policy on backlog per instance and configure a warm pool for pre-initialised capacity','Enable detailed monitoring only','C','Scaling on backlog per instance ties capacity to the actual queue of work rather than a lagging CPU average, and a warm pool removes the initialisation delay. A longer cooldown makes the group react more slowly, not faster.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional');

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Development with AWS Services','exam','A DynamoDB table has partition key customer_id and sort key order_date. The application must fetch the five most recent orders for one customer as cheaply as possible. What should the code do?','Query on the partition key with ScanIndexForward set to false and Limit 5','Scan the table and sort the results in the application','Query with a FilterExpression on order_date and Limit 5','Call GetItem with the customer id only','A','A Query on the partition key reads the sort key range in order, and reversing the scan direction with a limit returns the newest five while reading only five items. A Scan reads the whole table and a FilterExpression is applied after the read is charged.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Development with AWS Services','hard','Two concurrent Lambda invocations decrement the same stock counter and the item oversells. Which change prevents it without adding a lock service?','Increase the provisioned write capacity on the table','Perform both updates in a single BatchWriteItem call','Use UpdateItem with an ADD of minus one and a ConditionExpression that the stock is greater than zero, retrying on ConditionalCheckFailedException','Read the item, subtract in application code and call PutItem','C','A conditional atomic update is evaluated by DynamoDB against the current item, so the losing writer fails rather than overwriting. Read-modify-write in application code has a window between the read and the write in which the other invocation commits.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Development with AWS Services','exam','Browser clients must upload files directly to S3 without AWS credentials, limited to ten minutes and to one key prefix. What should the backend return?','The bucket access point alias','A presigned POST whose policy restricts the key prefix, with an expiry of 600 seconds','Temporary root credentials from STS','A public bucket URL with a public write ACL','B','A presigned POST carries a signed policy that constrains the key, the content length and the expiry, so the browser can upload only what the policy permits. Handing out credentials or opening the bucket for public writes removes that constraint entirely.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Development with AWS Services','exam','A workflow calls three services in sequence, must retry a throttled step with exponential backoff and take a different path on failure, with no servers to manage. What should be used?','A single Lambda function calling the three services with sleeps between them','A chain of three SQS queues','EventBridge rules chained together by custom events','A Step Functions state machine with Retry and Catch configured on the task states','D','Step Functions expresses retry with backoff and error specific catch paths declaratively and keeps the execution history for each run. Sleeping inside a Lambda pays for idle time and loses all state if the invocation dies.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Security','exam','A function needs a database password that is rotated every 30 days with no code deployment. What should the developer use?','An environment variable encrypted with a KMS key','A String type parameter in Parameter Store','A file in S3 read at cold start','AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation, fetched and cached by the function','D','Secrets Manager rotates the credential and updates the stored version, so the function retrieves the current value at runtime and never ships a password. An encrypted environment variable is fixed at deployment time and a String parameter is not encrypted at all.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Security','hard','An S3 bucket in another account is configured to trigger a Lambda function, but invocations fail with AccessDenied even though the function execution role allows the S3 read. What is missing?','A resource based policy on the function allowing s3.amazonaws.com to invoke it, with SourceAccount and SourceArn conditions','A KMS grant for the bucket key','A VPC interface endpoint for S3','An IAM user representing the bucket','A','The execution role governs what the function may do; whether S3 may invoke the function is governed by the function resource based policy. The source account and source arn conditions are what stop a confused deputy invocation from another bucket.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Security','exam','A mobile app must let users sign in with an email and password and then call API Gateway with per user authorization. Which is correct?','A Cognito identity pool using unauthenticated identities','An API key issued per user at signup','A Cognito user pool with an API Gateway JWT or Cognito authorizer validating the token on each request','An IAM user created for every application user','C','A user pool is the identity store and issues signed JWTs that the API Gateway authorizer validates and passes to the backend as claims. API keys identify a caller for throttling and are explicitly not an authentication mechanism.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Security','exam','A developer must encrypt 200 MB objects on the client with a key that is never sent to AWS in plaintext. Which approach is correct?','Server side encryption with S3 managed keys','Envelope encryption: call KMS GenerateDataKey, encrypt the payload locally with the plaintext data key, discard it, and store the encrypted data key beside the object','Call the KMS Encrypt API with the 200 MB payload','SSE-KMS with S3 Bucket Keys enabled','B','KMS Encrypt accepts at most 4 KB, so bulk data must be encrypted locally under a data key that KMS wraps. Server side options mean AWS performs the encryption, which the requirement excludes.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Deployment','exam','A team wants to deploy a serverless application from a template, with a guided first deployment and a managed artifact bucket. Which command sequence is correct?','aws cloudformation create-stack with the raw template','sam build followed by sam deploy --guided','aws lambda update-function-code for each function','cdk synth followed by uploading the output in the console','B','sam build packages the dependencies and sam deploy --guided prompts for the stack name, Region and capabilities and stores the answers in the configuration file. Updating function code directly bypasses the stack and leaves CloudFormation drifted.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Deployment','hard','Ten percent of production traffic must reach a new function version, with instant rollback if the error rate rises. What is the correct mechanism?','Publish the version and change an environment variable on the function','Deploy the new code as a second function and split traffic with Route 53','Update the $LATEST code and watch the metrics','Publish a version and set a weighted routing configuration on the production alias, driven by a CodeDeploy canary deployment with a CloudWatch alarm for rollback','D','A Lambda alias supports weighted routing between two published versions, and CodeDeploy shifts the weight on a schedule and reverts on an alarm. $LATEST is mutable and cannot be split by weight.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Deployment','exam','An Elastic Beanstalk deployment must never reduce capacity, must not deploy onto instances currently serving traffic, and the team accepts extra cost. Which deployment policy fits?','Immutable','Rolling','Rolling with an additional batch','All at once','A','Immutable deployments launch a full parallel set of new instances in the same group and swap them in only after they pass health checks, so serving capacity is never reduced. Rolling policies update instances that are in service.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Deployment','exam','A pipeline builds a container image and must deploy it to ECS in a way that cannot silently change afterwards. What should the build do?','Tag the image latest and push it','Push the image and record the tag in a text file','Push the image and reference it in the task definition by repository digest','Build the image on the container instance','C','A digest is the immutable content hash of the image, so the task definition always launches exactly the bytes that were tested. A mutable tag such as latest can be repointed at different content after the deployment.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Troubleshooting and Optimization','exam','A developer needs to know which downstream call inside a Lambda function consumes most of the duration. What is the least effort?','Add print statements around every call','Enable enhanced CloudWatch metrics','Enable active tracing on the function and instrument the AWS SDK with the X-Ray SDK so each call appears as a subsegment','Increase the memory and compare the durations','C','Active tracing plus SDK instrumentation records a timed subsegment for every downstream call with no bespoke logging. Print statements produce unstructured output that still has to be correlated by hand.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Troubleshooting and Optimization','hard','A VPC attached function shows occasional multi second initialisation and cannot reach a third party API on the internet. Which pair of fixes is correct?','Move the function out of the VPC and add a NAT gateway','Keep the function in private subnets with a route to a NAT gateway for egress, and use provisioned concurrency to remove the initialisation latency','Add an internet gateway route to the private subnet and raise the timeout','Give the function a public IP address and enable Multi-AZ','B','A VPC attached function has no public address, so outbound internet access requires a NAT gateway reachable from the private subnet route table. Provisioned concurrency keeps initialised environments warm, which removes the cold start the team is seeing.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Troubleshooting and Optimization','exam','A provisioned capacity DynamoDB table throttles although total consumed capacity is far below the provisioned total. What is the most likely cause?','The table has no local secondary index','The read capacity units are set too high','Eventually consistent reads have been disabled','A hot partition, because one partition key value receives most of the traffic and throughput is bounded per partition','D','Provisioned throughput is spread across partitions, so a single dominant key can saturate its partition while the table average stays low. The remedy is a higher cardinality partition key or write sharding.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Troubleshooting and Optimization','exam','An API Gateway proxy integration to Lambda returns 502 Bad Gateway. What should the developer check first?','That the function returns an object containing statusCode and a string body, as proxy integration requires','That caching is enabled on the stage','That the usage plan has a quota configured','That the Lambda timeout is below 29 seconds','A','With proxy integration API Gateway rejects any response that does not match the required shape and reports it as 502. A timeout would surface as 504 and throttling as 429.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Development with AWS Services','exam','A worker takes up to eight minutes on some SQS messages and the same message is repeatedly processed twice. Which change fixes it?','Set the visibility timeout above the maximum processing time, or extend it with ChangeMessageVisibility while the work is in progress','Enable content based deduplication','Switch from long polling to short polling','Reduce the message retention period','A','A message becomes visible again when the visibility timeout expires, so a timeout shorter than the work guarantees a second delivery while the first worker is still running. Deduplication applies only to FIFO queues at enqueue time.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Development with AWS Services','hard','Events must be routed to different targets based on fields nested inside the JSON payload, with the ability to archive and replay them. Which service should be used?','SNS with message attribute filter policies','SQS with a filter policy','Kinesis with a Lambda function acting as a router','EventBridge with content based rules matching on the detail fields, plus an archive and replay','D','EventBridge matches on the event body itself rather than on separate attributes, and provides native archive and replay. SNS filter policies read only message attributes, and SQS has no filtering at all.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Development with AWS Services','exam','A containerised service on ECS opens a PostgreSQL connection per request and latency climbs under load. What is the correct code level fix?','Increase the task CPU allocation','Use a connection pool inside the process with a bounded maximum, so connections are reused across requests','Open the connection on a background thread','Enable TLS so the handshake completes faster','B','Each new PostgreSQL connection forks a backend process on the server, which is expensive, so a bounded in process pool amortises that cost across requests. More CPU on the task does not remove the per connection server side setup.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Development with AWS Services','exam','An SQS triggered Lambda must not apply the same payment twice when a message is redelivered. Which is correct?','Delete the message before processing it','Set the batch size to one','Record the payment identifier in DynamoDB with a conditional put and skip the work when the item already exists','Use a FIFO queue and rely on the five minute deduplication window','C','A conditional put on a unique identifier gives a durable, atomic record of work already done that survives retries and restarts. The FIFO deduplication interval covers only five minutes and does not protect against a later redelivery.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Security','exam','A call to kms:Decrypt fails with AccessDeniedException although the calling role has an IAM policy allowing it. What is the likely cause?','The key is in another Region and needs a multi Region alias','An SCP exception is required for KMS','The key policy does not grant access to the role and does not delegate authorisation to IAM in that account','The ciphertext blob was base64 encoded','C','A KMS key is authorised by its key policy first; an identity policy grants nothing unless the key policy allows the account or the principal. This is why the default key policy contains a statement enabling IAM policies in the owning account.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Security','hard','A partner must upload to one S3 prefix for one hour and must not have an IAM principal in the account. What is correct?','Have the partner call sts:AssumeRole on a role that trusts the partner account with an ExternalId, using a session policy that limits the prefix and a one hour session duration','Create an IAM user and email the access keys','Make the prefix publicly writable for one hour','Share the account root credentials through Secrets Manager','A','Cross account role assumption issues short lived credentials, and the ExternalId defends against the confused deputy while the session policy narrows the session to the prefix. Long lived keys and public write both leave access open beyond the hour.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Security','exam','A configuration value is sensitive, needs no rotation, and must cost as little as possible. What should be used?','Secrets Manager with rotation disabled','A plaintext Lambda environment variable','A DynamoDB table with encryption at rest','A SecureString parameter in SSM Parameter Store encrypted with a KMS key','D','SecureString parameters give KMS encryption and IAM controlled retrieval at no per secret charge in the standard tier. Secrets Manager charges per secret per month, which is unnecessary when rotation is not required.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Security','exam','A function must read one DynamoDB table and write to one S3 prefix. Which policy follows least privilege?','The AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess and AmazonS3FullAccess managed policies','An inline policy allowing dynamodb:GetItem and dynamodb:Query on that table ARN and s3:PutObject on the bucket ARN restricted to the prefix','A policy allowing all actions with an explicit deny on iam','The AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole alone','B','Least privilege names the specific actions and the specific resource ARNs the function actually needs. Broad managed policies grant access to every table and bucket in the account, and the basic execution role grants only logging.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Deployment','exam','A pipeline must run unit tests before deploying and require a human approval before production. Which configuration is correct?','One stage with build and deploy actions running in parallel','A source stage, a build stage that runs the tests, a deploy to staging stage, a manual approval action, then a production deploy stage','A build stage that deploys straight to production when the tests pass','Two separate pipelines triggered on a schedule','B','CodePipeline enforces ordering between stages and a manual approval action blocks the transition until a human approves. Parallel build and deploy would let an untested artifact reach the environment.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Deployment','hard','A CloudFormation update unexpectedly replaced an RDS instance. How should the team have detected this before applying?','Run cfn-lint over the template','Enable termination protection on the stack','Create a change set and inspect the Replacement column for every affected resource','Run drift detection after the update','C','A change set describes each resource action and states explicitly whether the update requires replacement, before anything is executed. Drift detection reports differences after the fact and linting does not evaluate resource level update behaviour.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Deployment','exam','Several functions share a 60 MB dependency set and deployment packages are slow to upload. What should the team do?','Publish the dependencies as a Lambda layer and attach it to each function','Increase the function memory','Download the dependencies from S3 on every invocation','Merge all the functions into one','A','A layer is uploaded once and mounted into each function environment, so the individual deployment packages contain only the handler code. Downloading dependencies per invocation adds latency to every request.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Deployment','exam','The same build artifact must run in dev, staging and production with different table names, without rebuilding. What is the correct approach?','Hardcode the names and branch on the git branch','Produce one artifact per environment','Keep the names in code behind feature flags','Read the names from environment variables or from Parameter Store paths scoped per stage','D','Configuration supplied at runtime keeps one immutable artifact promotable across environments, which is what makes the tested build the deployed build. Rebuilding per environment means production runs bytes that were never tested.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Troubleshooting and Optimization','exam','A developer must count 500 responses per hour over the last two days from a function log group. What is the most direct tool?','Download the log streams and grep them','Create a metric filter, which will then cover the past period','X-Ray analytics','CloudWatch Logs Insights with a stats count query bucketed by time','D','Logs Insights queries the retained log data directly, including data written before the query existed, and aggregates by time bucket. A metric filter only creates data points from the moment it is created.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Troubleshooting and Optimization','hard','An endpoint backed by PostgreSQL slowed from 20 ms to 2 s after a release added a filter on tenant_id with the newest 20 rows by creation time. EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows a sort over two million rows. What is the correct fix?','Add one index on tenant_id and a separate index on the timestamp','Add a composite btree index on tenant_id and the timestamp in descending order so the ordering is satisfied by the index and only 20 rows are read','Increase work_mem so the sort fits in memory','Move the LIMIT into a subquery','B','A composite index whose leading column matches the equality predicate and whose second column matches the sort order lets the planner walk the index and stop after 20 rows. Two separate indexes still require a sort of all matching rows.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Troubleshooting and Optimization','exam','A client receives bursts of ProvisionedThroughputExceededException and retries immediately in a tight loop. What should be implemented?','A fixed one second retry loop','Retry once and then fail','Exponential backoff with jitter, using the SDK retry configuration','A longer socket timeout','C','Backoff with jitter spreads retries over time so the client stops amplifying the overload it is reacting to. Immediate or fixed interval retries synchronise clients and keep the service saturated.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Troubleshooting and Optimization','exam','A Lambda writes items to DynamoDB one at a time and its duration is dominated by request round trips. Which change gives the biggest improvement?','Use BatchWriteItem to send up to 25 items per request and handle UnprocessedItems','Increase the memory to 3008 MB','Wrap all the writes in a single transaction','Enable DAX for the writes','A','Batching removes most of the network round trips, which is where the time is actually spent. Transactions add coordination cost and DAX is a read cache that does not accelerate writes.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Development with AWS Services','exam','A thumbnail must be produced whenever an image lands in a bucket, once per object and without polling. Which design is correct?','An S3 event notification to an SQS queue consumed by a Lambda function, with idempotent processing','A cron driven Lambda that lists the bucket every minute','A job that reads CloudTrail lookups','An S3 lifecycle rule','A','S3 event notifications push the object key as it is created, and the queue absorbs bursts and gives a dead letter path for failures. Listing the bucket on a schedule adds latency and cost and scales badly as the bucket grows.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Development with AWS Services','hard','An order write and its audit record must both succeed or both fail inside DynamoDB. What should the developer use?','BatchWriteItem, which is atomic across items','TransactWriteItems, which applies all of its actions atomically within one Region','Two PutItem calls with a retry around them','A Step Functions parallel state','B','TransactWriteItems is all or nothing across up to 100 items in one Region, which is exactly the requirement. BatchWriteItem is explicitly not atomic and can partially succeed, returning unprocessed items.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Security','exam','An internal API must accept calls only from a specific VPC and reject all internet traffic. Which is correct?','A regional API with a WAF rule allowing the NAT gateway address','An edge optimised API protected by an API key','A private REST API reached through an interface VPC endpoint, with a resource policy that allows only that endpoint id','An HTTP API with a Lambda authorizer that checks the source IP','C','A private API has no public endpoint at all and the resource policy pins access to the named VPC endpoint. IP based controls on a public endpoint still expose the API to the internet and can be spoofed or misconfigured.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Security','exam','A review finds that a function logs the entire event, which contains cardholder data. What is the correct remediation?','Reduce the log retention to one day','Encrypt the log group with KMS and keep logging the event','Move the logs to S3','Stop logging the sensitive fields by redacting them before the log call, and add a CloudWatch Logs data protection policy as a backstop','D','The defect is that the data is written at all, so the fix is to remove it at the source and add a detection and masking policy in case another path logs it. Retention, encryption and relocation all still store the cardholder data.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Deployment','exam','A CloudFormation update fails part way and the stack is left in UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED. What is the correct recovery?','Delete the stack immediately','Create a new stack with the same resources','Wait for the automatic retry','Resolve whatever blocks the rollback, then call ContinueUpdateRollback, optionally skipping the resource that cannot roll back','D','ContinueUpdateRollback resumes the rollback once the blocking condition is cleared, and the skip list lets a stuck resource be excluded and reconciled afterwards. Deleting the stack destroys the resources it manages.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Deployment','hard','A blue green release must change a PostgreSQL column type while both versions run. Which practice keeps it safe?','Apply the change in the deployment transaction and lock the table','Recreate the table with the new type and copy the rows during cutover','Expand and contract: add the new column, write to both, backfill, switch reads, and drop the old column in a later release','Take the application offline for the migration','C','Expand and contract keeps the schema compatible with both the old and the new code at every moment, which is what a blue green cutover requires. A single destructive change breaks whichever version is not yet or no longer deployed.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Troubleshooting and Optimization','exam','Clients receive 429 responses although Lambda concurrency is barely used. Where should the developer look?','The reserved concurrency on the function','The API Gateway stage or usage plan throttling rate and burst settings','The VPC endpoint policy','The CloudFront cache behaviour','B','API Gateway throttles at the account, stage and usage plan level and returns 429 before the integration is ever invoked, which is consistent with idle Lambda concurrency. A Lambda concurrency limit would show as throttles on the function metric.'),
('AWS Certified Developer - Associate','Troubleshooting and Optimization','exam','A CPU bound function takes four seconds at 512 MB. The team wants both lower latency and lower cost. What should they do?','Benchmark higher memory settings, because CPU is allocated in proportion to memory and a shorter duration can cost the same or less','Lower the memory to 128 MB to reduce the price per millisecond','Split the function into two sequential functions','Move the function into a VPC','A','Lambda scales vCPU with the memory setting, so a CPU bound function often finishes more than proportionally faster, leaving the GB-second cost flat or lower. Reducing memory makes a CPU bound function slower and usually more expensive.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'AWS Certified Developer - Associate');

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Preliminary Phase','exam','An organization is establishing its first architecture capability. Which activity belongs to the Preliminary Phase?','Producing the Architecture Definition Document','Defining the Target Business Architecture','Creating the Implementation and Migration Plan','Tailoring the ADM to the organization and establishing the architecture governance framework, the architecture principles and the tools','D','The Preliminary Phase prepares the organization to do architecture at all: it defines where, what, why, who and how architecture will be done. The named deliverables in the other options are produced inside the ADM cycle that the Preliminary Phase sets up.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase A: Architecture Vision','exam','Which deliverable is approved at the end of Phase A and authorizes the architecture work to proceed?','The Architecture Definition Document','The Statement of Architecture Work, signed by the sponsor','The Architecture Contract with the implementers','The Request for Architecture Work','B','The Statement of Architecture Work defines the scope, constraints, resources and schedule of the engagement and is signed off by the sponsor at the end of Phase A. The Request for Architecture Work is the input that triggers Phase A, not its output.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase A: Architecture Vision','exam','A CIO asks for a concise summary of the target state that business executives can grasp at the start of a cycle. Which artifact is intended for that?','The Architecture Vision, supported by a Solution Concept or Value Chain diagram','The Technology Portfolio catalog','The Application Communication diagram','The Architecture Requirements Specification','A','The Architecture Vision is deliberately high level and aimed at gaining executive commitment before detailed work begins. The other artifacts are domain level detail produced in later phases for practitioner audiences.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Requirements Management','hard','How does Requirements Management relate to the ADM phases?','It is executed once between Phase A and Phase B','It replaces the Architecture Repository','It is a continuous process at the centre of the ADM that every phase interacts with, storing and managing requirements while the phases themselves address and dispose of them','It is performed only in Phase G by the implementation governance board','C','Requirements Management sits at the hub of the ADM and operates throughout, but it does not resolve requirements itself; the relevant ADM phase addresses each one. That is why the ADM diagram shows every phase connected to the centre.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase B: Business Architecture','exam','A team must show which organizational units perform which business functions before any system design begins. Which Phase B artifact is appropriate?','A Business Footprint or Functional Decomposition diagram, supported by the Organization/Actor catalog','The Data Entity/Data Component catalog','The Environments and Locations diagram','The Implementation and Migration Plan','A','These are Business Architecture artifacts that map functions to organizational units and actors. The data catalog and environments diagram belong to Phase C and Phase D respectively.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase B: Business Architecture','exam','Why does TOGAF recommend developing the Business Architecture before the other architecture domains?','Because business stakeholders control the technology budget','Because the Enterprise Continuum requires that order','Because it demonstrates the business value of the work to stakeholders and supplies the drivers and requirements that shape the data, application and technology architectures','Because the ADM forbids working on domains in parallel','C','Business Architecture is the prerequisite that gives the later domains their purpose and their traceable requirements. TOGAF explicitly allows phases to be iterated and partially parallelised, so the reason is dependency, not prohibition.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase C: Information Systems Architectures','exam','In Phase C, which pair of artifacts records which applications create and consume which data entities?','The Technology Standards catalog and the Networked Computing diagram','The Business Service/Information diagram and the Goal/Objective/Service diagram','The Project Context diagram and the Benefits diagram','The Data Entity/Data Component catalog and the Application/Data matrix','D','The catalog inventories the data entities and the matrix maps them to the applications that create, read, update and delete them. The other pairs belong to Phase D, Phase B and Phase E.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase C: Information Systems Architectures','hard','An architect must decide whether to develop the Data Architecture or the Application Architecture first within Phase C. What does the ADM say?','Data must always be developed first','Either order is permitted and the sequence is a tailoring decision, with data first common when data is the primary driver','Applications must always be developed first','Phase C is omitted when a package will be purchased','B','TOGAF states that the order within Phase C depends on the situation and the business drivers, and that the two are often developed iteratively. Buying a package changes how the architecture is realised, not whether it is described.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase D: Technology Architecture','exam','Which Phase D output records the concrete platform services and standards chosen for the target state?','The Business Interaction matrix','The Architecture Vision','The Technology Portfolio catalog and Technology Standards catalog, feeding the Technology Architecture section of the Architecture Definition Document','The Implementation Governance Model','C','Phase D produces the technology building blocks and the standards that constrain them, recorded in those catalogs and consolidated into the Architecture Definition Document. The Architecture Vision precedes all domain detail.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase E: Opportunities and Solutions','exam','What is the primary purpose of Phase E?','To sign the Architecture Contract with the implementers','To gather the initial business requirements','To manage changes to the deployed architecture','To identify delivery vehicles, consolidate gaps into work packages, group them into Transition Architectures and perform the initial implementation planning','D','Phase E is where the architecture first becomes a plan: gaps become work packages and work packages become candidate projects and transition states. The Architecture Contract belongs to Phase G and change management to Phase H.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase E: Opportunities and Solutions','exam','A gap analysis has produced 90 individual gaps. What does Phase E do with them?','Escalates each gap to the Architecture Board separately','Consolidates them into work packages and candidate projects, then groups those into Transition Architectures','Discards any gap that no project will fund','Converts each gap into an architecture principle','B','Phase E aggregates gaps into deliverable units of work so that implementation can be planned and sequenced. Discarding unfunded gaps would silently change the target architecture without governance.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase F: Migration Planning','hard','Which Phase F activity determines the order in which the identified projects are executed?','Prioritising the projects through a business value assessment and cost benefit analysis, confirming the Transition Architecture sequence and finalising the Implementation and Migration Plan','Producing the Architecture Vision','Issuing the Request for Architecture Work','Performing compliance reviews of the implementation projects','A','Phase F turns the Phase E project list into a resourced, prioritised and costed plan aligned with the enterprise portfolio process. Compliance reviews of running projects are a Phase G activity.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase G: Implementation Governance','exam','Which document binds an implementation project to the agreed architecture in Phase G?','The Statement of Architecture Work','The Architecture Contract','The Request for Architecture Work','The Architecture Definition Document','B','The Architecture Contract is the joint agreement between the architecture function and the implementing organization on how the architecture will be realised and monitored. The Statement of Architecture Work governs the architecture engagement itself, not the implementation.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase G: Implementation Governance','exam','An implementation team proposes to deviate from a mandated technology standard. What is the governed response?','Raise it through an architecture compliance review, and if the deviation is accepted record a time limited dispensation granted by the Architecture Board','Allow it quietly and correct it in the next cycle','Halt the project and restart the ADM at Phase A','Amend the Architecture Vision without a review','A','Governance handles deviations explicitly: they are reviewed, and an accepted deviation becomes a recorded dispensation with an expiry so that it is revisited. Silent deviation destroys the traceability that governance exists to maintain.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase H: Architecture Change Management','exam','A new regulation invalidates part of the deployed target architecture. What does Phase H prescribe?','A simplification change handled by change management alone','An incremental change funded from the operating budget','A re-architecting change, which issues a new Request for Architecture Work and returns to the beginning of the ADM cycle','No action until the next scheduled review','C','A change that invalidates the architecture itself exceeds the scope of change management and requires a new architecture cycle. Simplification and incremental changes are for value delivery within the existing architecture.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase H: Architecture Change Management','hard','How does TOGAF classify a change that delivers additional value from the existing architecture without altering its fundamental structure?','A re-architecting change','A dispensation','A capability increment that always triggers a new ADM cycle','A simplification or incremental change, handled through the change management process rather than a new ADM cycle','D','Phase H distinguishes simplification and incremental changes, which are absorbed by change management, from re-architecting changes, which restart the cycle. The trigger for a new cycle is structural invalidation, not the mere existence of change.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Architecture Content Framework','exam','In the content framework, what distinguishes a deliverable from an artifact?','Artifacts are contractual and deliverables are internal working papers','They are two words for the same thing','A deliverable is always a diagram and an artifact is always a document','A deliverable is a contractually specified work product that is formally reviewed and signed off, whereas an artifact is a finer grained work product such as a catalog, matrix or diagram, and deliverables contain artifacts','D','Deliverables are the units of formal agreement and sign off; artifacts are the granular descriptions that populate them and the repository. Building blocks are the third concept, representing the components the artifacts describe.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Architecture Content Framework','exam','A building block that describes a required capability without naming a product is which type?','An Architecture Building Block','A Solution Building Block','A deliverable','A dispensation','A','Architecture Building Blocks specify capability and shape the specification of Solution Building Blocks, which are the implementations that realise them. That separation is what allows a target architecture to survive a change of product.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Architecture Content Framework','exam','Which of the following is a matrix rather than a catalog or a diagram in the content metamodel?','The Technology Standards catalog','The Application/Data matrix','The Application Communication diagram','The Organization/Actor catalog','B','Matrices record relationships between two sets of metamodel entities, which is exactly what the Application/Data matrix does. Catalogs list entities of one type and diagrams present them pictorially for a stakeholder concern.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Architecture Content Framework','hard','Which statement about the content metamodel extensions is correct?','All extensions must be adopted for the metamodel to be valid','Extensions replace the core metamodel entities','The core metamodel is extended by optional modules such as governance, services, process modelling, data, infrastructure consolidation and motivation, applied only where the enterprise needs the additional detail','Extensions may be applied only in Phase D','C','The extensions are modular and selective, so an enterprise adds only the detail its concerns require and avoids modelling overhead elsewhere. They add to the core rather than replacing it.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Enterprise Continuum','exam','What is the Enterprise Continuum?','A repository product sold by The Open Group','A list of approved technology vendors','A classification for categorising architecture and solution artifacts as they evolve from generic Foundation Architectures through to organization specific architectures','A phase of the ADM','C','The Enterprise Continuum is a view for classifying and relating reusable assets by their degree of specificity to the enterprise. It is a classification scheme, not a tool and not a phase.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Enterprise Continuum','exam','What is the correct order of the Architecture Continuum from most generic to most specific?','Organization Specific, Industry, Common Systems, Foundation','Foundation Architectures, Common Systems Architectures, Industry Architectures, Organization Specific Architectures','Industry, Foundation, Organization Specific, Common Systems','Common Systems, Foundation, Organization Specific, Industry','B','The continuum runs from universally applicable foundations, through architectures common to many systems, to industry specific and finally enterprise specific architectures. Assets are both specialised leftwards to rightwards and generalised back for reuse.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Enterprise Continuum','exam','A reference model for an integrated information infrastructure aimed at Boundaryless Information Flow is which of the following?','The Technical Reference Model','The Architecture Repository','The Standards Information Base','The Integrated Information Infrastructure Reference Model','D','The III-RM is the TOGAF reference model addressing application level integration and Boundaryless Information Flow. The TRM describes the generic platform services beneath applications.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Enterprise Continuum','hard','Where in the Architecture Repository are the specific standards the enterprise has mandated recorded?','The Standards Information Base','The Architecture Landscape','The Governance Log','The Reference Library','A','The Standards Information Base holds the standards the enterprise has adopted and with which new architectures must comply. The Reference Library holds guidelines and reference material, and the Governance Log records decisions and dispensations.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','ADM Guidelines and Techniques','exam','An enterprise has 12 semi autonomous business units at differing maturity levels. Which TOGAF technique addresses applying the ADM at different scopes and levels of detail?','Applying iteration to the ADM together with architecture partitioning by level of detail, subject matter and time period','The Business Transformation Readiness Assessment','Gap analysis','The Architecture Contract','A','Iteration and partitioning are the techniques for running several architecture efforts at different depths and scopes without them colliding. Readiness assessment and gap analysis answer different questions inside a single effort.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','ADM Guidelines and Techniques','exam','Which technique establishes whether the organization is capable of absorbing the change the architecture requires?','Gap analysis','Risk management','Capability based planning','The Business Transformation Readiness Assessment','D','The readiness assessment evaluates organizational factors such as sponsorship, culture, funding and skills before the transformation is committed. Gap analysis measures the distance between baseline and target, not the ability to travel it.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','ADM Guidelines and Techniques','exam','TOGAF classifies architecture risk before and after mitigation. What are those two levels called?','Inherent and accepted','Primary and secondary','Initial level of risk and residual level of risk','Gross and net','C','TOGAF assesses initial risk, applies mitigation, then reassesses the residual risk, which is what governance actually approves. Residual risk that remains unacceptable must be escalated rather than absorbed.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','ADM Guidelines and Techniques','hard','In a gap analysis matrix with baseline building blocks on one axis and target building blocks on the other, what does a baseline row with no target match indicate?','A new building block that must be introduced','A baseline building block that is being eliminated, which must be a deliberate decision rather than an oversight','A building block that is unchanged between baseline and target','A duplicated building block','B','An unmatched baseline row means the capability disappears in the target, and TOGAF requires that this be an explicit and justified decision. An unmatched target column, by contrast, is the new capability to be built or bought.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Architecture Governance','exam','Which body approves dispensations and oversees compliance across implementation projects?','The project steering committee','The Architecture Board','The Enterprise Continuum','The Requirements Management process','B','The Architecture Board is the cross organization body accountable for architecture governance, compliance and dispensations. The Enterprise Continuum is a classification scheme and has no decision authority.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Architecture Governance','exam','A solution implements only some features of the specification, and every feature it does implement follows the specification. Which TOGAF compliance level is this?','Fully conformant','Non conformant','Compliant','Irrelevant','C','TOGAF grades compliance as irrelevant, consistent, compliant, conformant and fully conformant; compliant means some features are unimplemented but nothing implemented contradicts the specification. Fully conformant requires complete correspondence with the specification.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Architecture Governance','exam','Which characteristic of architecture governance ensures decisions and their justification remain available for later review?','Transparency and accountability, supported by a governance log that records decisions and their rationale','Discipline alone','Confidentiality of all architecture decisions','Delegation of every decision to project managers','A','Governance depends on decisions being visible and attributable, which the governance log provides. Without a recorded rationale a later team cannot tell a deliberate trade off from an accident.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Architecture Governance','hard','Using the concepts TOGAF adopts from ISO/IEC 42010, what is the relationship between a stakeholder concern, a viewpoint and a view?','A view defines the viewpoint that a stakeholder will use','Concerns are derived from the views produced in Phase G','Viewpoints and views are interchangeable terms','A viewpoint specifies the conventions for constructing a view that addresses particular stakeholder concerns, and the view is the resulting representation of the system','D','The viewpoint is the specification, the view is the instance produced from it, and both exist to address identified stakeholder concerns. Confusing the two is why architecture documents often present diagrams no stakeholder asked for.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase B: Business Architecture','exam','Which technique helps an enterprise plan around what it is able to do rather than around what it owns, and to size business increments accordingly?','Gap analysis','The Technical Reference Model','Migration planning techniques','Capability based planning','D','Capability based planning organises the architecture and its increments around business capabilities and their target maturity. It is the technique that connects business outcomes to the work packages that deliver them.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase D: Technology Architecture','exam','A mandated cloud first rule must constrain every technology decision. Where is it formally recorded and validated?','In the Architecture Contract only','In the Implementation and Migration Plan','As an architecture principle established in the Preliminary Phase, with a name, statement, rationale and implications, tested against the criteria of understandability, robustness, completeness, consistency and stability','In the Standards Information Base only','C','Architecture principles are the enduring rules that constrain decisions across cycles, and TOGAF gives both a standard format and quality criteria for them. Contracts and plans apply principles rather than establish them.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase E: Opportunities and Solutions','exam','A Transition Architecture is best described as which of the following?','A formally described enterprise architecture state at an architecturally significant intermediate point between the baseline and the target','A rejected design option retained for the record','The vendor solution selected for a work package','A view produced specifically for the CIO','A','Transition Architectures are intermediate target states that let a long programme deliver business value in stages. Each is a described architecture in its own right, not merely a milestone date.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Requirements Management','hard','An architecture team has completed Phase D and discovers that an assumption made in Phase B is wrong. What does the ADM prescribe?','Continue into Phase E and correct the assumption in the next cycle','Return to the affected phase, because the ADM is iterative and the change is fed back through Requirements Management, then re-validate the downstream phases','Raise a dispensation with the Architecture Board and proceed unchanged','Restart the entire cycle from the Preliminary Phase','B','The ADM is explicitly iterative and Requirements Management routes a changed requirement back to the phase that must address it, after which the dependent phases are revisited. Carrying a known false assumption forward invalidates every downstream decision built on it.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Architecture Content Framework','exam','How do the Architecture Definition Document and the Architecture Requirements Specification differ?','The Architecture Definition Document is the qualitative narrative and models of the architecture, while the Architecture Requirements Specification carries the quantitative requirements and the criteria an implementation must meet','They are two names for the same deliverable','The Architecture Requirements Specification is produced only in Phase G','The Architecture Definition Document contains only the migration plan','A','The two are companion deliverables: one describes what the architecture is, the other states measurably what it must satisfy. The requirements specification is what an implementation is later assessed against.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Enterprise Continuum','exam','A Solution Building Block taken from a vendor product catalog sits where in the Enterprise Continuum?','In the Architecture Continuum under Foundation Architectures','In the Solutions Continuum, which is populated with implementations that realise the corresponding Architecture Building Blocks','In the Governance Log','Outside the Enterprise Continuum','B','The Enterprise Continuum has two parallel halves, and products and implementations belong to the Solutions Continuum while capability specifications belong to the Architecture Continuum. Each Solution Building Block realises one or more Architecture Building Blocks.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Architecture Governance','exam','What is the purpose of an architecture compliance review, and when is it best scheduled?','To audit the vendor contract after go live','To substitute for system testing','To assess a project against the architecture early enough that the findings can still change the design, typically at project inception and at major milestones','To approve the Request for Architecture Work','C','A compliance review is worth running only while the design can still be changed, which is why TOGAF places it early and repeats it at milestones. A review after go live can record a finding but cannot prevent the cost.'),
('TOGAF 9 Certified','Phase A: Architecture Vision','hard','A stakeholder map places a regulator in the high power, low interest quadrant for the daily programme. How should the engagement treat that stakeholder?','Ignore the regulator because the interest level is low','Give the regulator the same detailed views as the delivery teams','Delegate the relationship to the implementation project','Keep the regulator satisfied with targeted views addressing its specific concerns, because power determines the level of engagement required','D','TOGAF stakeholder management uses the power and interest position to set the engagement approach, and high power with low interest means keep satisfied with focused material. Flooding such a stakeholder with delivery detail is as damaging as ignoring it.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'TOGAF 9 Certified');

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Architecture','exam','Two sessions update different rows of the same table at the same time. Why does neither block the other?','Because PostgreSQL escalates to a table lock and serialises the statements','Because MVCC creates a new row version for each update and row level locks are taken only on the rows actually written','Because readers block writers in PostgreSQL','Because both updates run inside the same snapshot','B','PostgreSQL takes a row level exclusive lock only on tuples it modifies, and MVCC lets concurrent readers continue against older versions. Lock escalation to a table lock does not exist in PostgreSQL.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Architecture','hard','Monitoring shows the age of datfrozenxid approaching autovacuum_freeze_max_age and transaction ID wraparound warnings appear in the log. What is happening and what is the correct response?','The WAL directory is full and archiving must be disabled','There are too many connections and max_connections must be reduced','Statistics are stale and ANALYZE will clear the warning','Old tuples have not been frozen, so aggressive autovacuum must be allowed to finish, and anything holding back the transaction horizon must be cleared: long running transactions, abandoned prepared transactions, and unused replication slots','D','Freezing is blocked by whatever pins the oldest visible transaction id, so vacuum cannot advance the frozen horizon until those holders are released. Ignoring it ends in a forced shutdown to protect against wraparound.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Architecture','exam','Which background process writes dirty shared buffers out between checkpoints to smooth the I/O pattern?','The WAL writer','The autovacuum launcher','The background writer','The archiver','C','The background writer trickles dirty buffers to the operating system so that backends find clean buffers and checkpoints have less to flush. The WAL writer flushes the WAL buffers, which is a different data path.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Architecture','exam','A dedicated server has 64 GB of RAM. Which shared_buffers setting follows the usual guidance, and why?','About 16 GB, roughly a quarter of RAM, leaving memory for the operating system page cache that PostgreSQL also depends on','60 GB, so that all data is held in shared buffers','256 MB, because the operating system cache is always faster','work_mem multiplied by max_connections','A','PostgreSQL uses a double buffering model, reading through the OS page cache, so allocating almost all RAM to shared buffers starves that second cache and wastes memory on duplication. Around 25 percent is the standard starting point, tuned by measurement.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Architecture','exam','What guarantees that a committed transaction survives an immediate crash?','The data files are written synchronously at commit','The background writer flushes the buffers before commit returns','Write ahead logging: the commit WAL record is flushed to durable storage before the commit is acknowledged to the client','A checkpoint is taken at the end of every transaction','C','WAL is written and flushed before the commit is acknowledged, so recovery can replay it and reconstruct the data pages. The heap pages themselves are written later by the background writer and checkpointer.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Architecture','hard','A heavily updated table carries 12 indexes and suffers write amplification and bloat, although the updated columns are never indexed. Which change helps most directly?','Lower the fillfactor on the table so pages keep free space, allowing heap only tuple updates that avoid writing to every index','Add an index on the frequently updated columns','Set autovacuum_enabled to off for that table','Increase maintenance_work_mem','A','A HOT update keeps the new tuple version on the same page and skips all index updates, but it requires free space on that page. Lowering the fillfactor reserves that space, which is exactly what converts these updates to HOT.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Architecture','exam','A table stores JSON documents averaging 200 KB each. Where are those values physically stored?','Inline in the heap page, which expands beyond 8 KB for the row','Compressed and split into chunks in the associated TOAST table, with a pointer left in the heap tuple','In pg_largeobject by default','In the WAL only','B','A tuple cannot span pages, so values over the toast threshold are compressed and, if still too large, moved out of line into the TOAST relation. That is why selecting a narrow column set from such a table is much cheaper than selecting the document.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','SQL','exam','A report needs the three most recent orders for each customer from a large orders table. Which construct is correct and efficient?','A correlated subquery returning the maximum date per customer','GROUP BY customer_id with LIMIT 3','SELECT DISTINCT with no ORDER BY','A window function computing row_number over a partition by customer ordered by the timestamp descending, filtered to rank three or lower in an outer query','D','row_number within a partition ranks rows per customer in one pass, and the outer filter keeps the top n per group. GROUP BY with LIMIT limits the number of groups, not the rows within each group.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','SQL','exam','A loader must insert a row, or update it when the natural key already exists, atomically and without a race. What should it use?','SELECT then INSERT or UPDATE in application code','INSERT wrapped in an exception handler inside a trigger','A plain INSERT with a check constraint','INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE, which relies on a unique index over the conflict target','D','ON CONFLICT resolves the collision inside the same statement using the unique index, so two concurrent loaders cannot both insert. A read followed by a write in application code leaves a window in which the other session commits.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','SQL','hard','A query using a NOT IN predicate against a subquery suddenly returns zero rows after a release. What is the most likely cause?','The child table was vacuumed','The subquery now returns NULL values, and NOT IN with a NULL in the list evaluates to unknown for every row, so nothing qualifies','The planner switched to a hash join','The id column changed type','B','NOT IN uses three valued logic, and a single NULL in the list makes the comparison unknown for every candidate row. NOT EXISTS or an anti join with an explicit null filter is the safe formulation.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','SQL','exam','Queries filter on the lower case form of an email column and never use the existing index on that column. What is the correct fix?','Create an index on the expression lower(email) and keep the query predicate in exactly the same form','Add a check constraint requiring the column to be lower case','Create a hash index on the column','Increase random_page_cost','A','An index on a plain column cannot serve a predicate on a function of that column; an expression index stores the computed value and matches the predicate. The expression in the index and in the query must be identical for the planner to use it.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','SQL','exam','For each product a query must return the two cheapest offers, using a subquery that depends on the outer row. Which construct is designed for this?','A CROSS JOIN followed by a filter','A recursive common table expression','A LATERAL join, which lets the subquery reference columns of the preceding FROM item','A UNION of one query per product','C','LATERAL is exactly the feature that allows a subquery in the FROM clause to see columns from the items to its left, so a per row top n query can be written once. Without it the correlation is not permitted in the FROM clause.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','SQL','exam','Inside one transaction a query reads a total, then reads it again and gets a different value. Which statement is correct?','This is expected under read committed, because each statement takes a fresh snapshot; repeatable read would give the whole transaction one stable snapshot','This indicates a bug in MVCC','Read committed in PostgreSQL permits dirty reads','Only serializable prevents this, repeatable read does not','A','Read committed, the default, takes a new snapshot per statement, so committed concurrent changes become visible mid transaction. Repeatable read fixes a single snapshot at the first statement and eliminates the non repeatable read.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','SQL','hard','An application using serializable isolation occasionally receives SQLSTATE 40001, could not serialize access. What must the application do?','Lower the isolation level to read uncommitted','Take an explicit table lock at the start of every transaction','Retry the entire transaction from the beginning, because serializable snapshot isolation detects dangerous read write dependencies and reports them as serialisation failures instead of blocking','Ignore the error, because the transaction actually committed','C','Serializable in PostgreSQL is optimistic: it lets transactions run and aborts one when the interleaving could not have occurred serially. A correct application therefore must implement a retry loop as part of using that isolation level.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Administration','exam','Which statement about roles is correct in PostgreSQL 12 and later?','Users and groups are separate object types','Only the postgres superuser may create a schema','A role with NOLOGIN cannot own objects','Users and groups are both roles, and membership together with the INHERIT attribute determines whether the member privileges apply automatically or require SET ROLE','D','PostgreSQL unified users and groups into roles, and a NOINHERIT member must issue SET ROLE to use the privileges of a role it belongs to. A NOLOGIN role is the normal way to model a group and can own objects.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Administration','hard','Row level security is enabled on a table with a policy, yet the table owner still sees every row. Why?','The policy syntax is wrong','Table owners bypass row level security unless FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY is set on the table','Row level security applies only to superusers','Row level security requires a SECURITY DEFINER function to take effect','B','By default the owner and superusers are exempt from the policies on their own tables, which surprises teams whose application connects as the owner. FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY makes the policies apply to the owner as well.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Administration','exam','A server with 8 cores is configured with max_connections of 2000 and shows heavy context switching and memory pressure under load. What is the correct remedy?','Raise max_connections to 5000 and increase work_mem','Put a pooler such as pgBouncer in transaction pooling mode in front of it and reduce max_connections to a small multiple of the core count','Disable autovacuum during peak hours','Switch to synchronous replication','B','Each connection is a backend process with its own memory, so thousands of them thrash the scheduler regardless of how little each does. A transaction mode pooler lets thousands of clients share a few dozen server connections.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Administration','hard','An index must be added to a busy 500 GB table without blocking writes. Which statement is correct?','Use CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY, accepting that it performs two table scans, cannot run inside a transaction block, and can leave an invalid index that must be dropped and rebuilt if it fails','A plain CREATE INDEX takes only a share lock and never blocks writes','Set lock_timeout to zero and run a plain CREATE INDEX','Use REINDEX, which is always an online operation','A','CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY takes a lock that permits concurrent inserts, updates and deletes, at the cost of two passes and a longer build. Its failure mode leaves an invalid index behind, which must be cleaned up explicitly.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Administration','exam','shared_preload_libraries has been changed in postgresql.conf. What is required for the change to take effect?','A reload with pg_ctl reload','Calling pg_reload_conf()','Nothing, it applies to the next new connection','A full server restart, because it is a postmaster level parameter','D','Parameters with the postmaster context are read only when the server starts, so a reload does not apply them. That is why installing an extension such as pg_stat_statements requires a maintenance window.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Administration','exam','A monitoring role must read the query text of all sessions in pg_stat_activity without being a superuser. What is the correct grant?','Make the role a superuser','Grant CONNECT on the database only','Grant the role membership of pg_read_all_stats, or of pg_monitor which includes it','Grant SELECT on pg_class','C','The default roles pg_read_all_stats and pg_monitor exist precisely so monitoring does not require superuser. Without them a non superuser sees its own queries and only the process metadata of others.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Administration','exam','A trigger must reject an update and return an error to the client without writing anything. What is correct?','An AFTER UPDATE row trigger returning NULL','A statement level BEFORE trigger returning NEW','A deferred constraint trigger returning OLD','A BEFORE UPDATE row level trigger that raises an exception, which aborts the statement and the surrounding transaction','D','Raising an exception in a BEFORE trigger stops the statement before any change is applied and propagates the error to the client. Returning NULL from an AFTER trigger has no effect, because the row has already been written.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Backup and Recovery','hard','A destructive DELETE ran at 14:32 and must be undone with the least possible data loss, given nightly base backups and continuous WAL archiving. What is the correct procedure?','Restore last night base backup and accept losing the day','Run pg_dump against a standby and reload the affected table','Restore the base backup into a new data directory, configure restore_command and a recovery target time just before 14:32, start the server in recovery and promote once the target is reached','Run pg_resetwal and restart the server','C','Point in time recovery replays archived WAL from the base backup up to the chosen target, so everything committed before 14:32 is preserved. pg_resetwal discards WAL and produces a corrupt, inconsistent cluster.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Backup and Recovery','exam','A 4 TB cluster must be backed up nightly with the shortest possible restore time and the ability to recover to a point in time. Which is correct?','A physical base backup taken with pg_basebackup, combined with continuous WAL archiving','pg_dump in custom format','pg_dumpall in plain SQL','COPY of every table to CSV files','A','A physical backup restores by copying files back rather than by re-executing SQL and rebuilding indexes, which is far faster at this size, and archived WAL supplies point in time recovery. Logical dumps offer no point in time recovery at all.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Backup and Recovery','exam','Which statement about pg_dump is correct?','It blocks writes on the tables being dumped for the duration of the dump','It runs inside a single repeatable read transaction, giving a consistent snapshot without blocking normal writes, and custom format dumps can be restored selectively and in parallel','It captures the WAL needed for point in time recovery','It dumps roles and tablespaces by default','B','pg_dump relies on MVCC for consistency rather than on locking out writers, and the custom format carries a table of contents that supports selective and parallel restore. Roles and tablespaces are cluster wide and require pg_dumpall.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Backup and Recovery','exam','The archive command has been failing for two days. What is the immediate operational risk?','The standby will be promoted automatically','Table statistics will become stale','WAL segments accumulate in pg_wal and can fill the filesystem, which stops the server, and the point in time recovery window now has a gap','Nothing, because WAL is recycled regardless','C','PostgreSQL refuses to recycle a WAL segment that has not been archived successfully, so a failing archive command turns into a disk full outage. The recovery window is simultaneously broken for the period that was never archived.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Backup and Recovery','hard','What is the strongest evidence that a backup strategy actually works?','The backup job exit code is zero','A periodic restore rehearsal into an isolated instance that reaches a consistent state and passes application level data checks, with the recovery time measured','The backup file grows each night','pg_verifybackup reports that the manifest is present','B','Only a restore proves that the backup is both complete and usable, and it also measures the recovery time objective against reality. Exit codes and manifest checks confirm that a file was produced, not that a business can be recovered from it.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Backup and Recovery','exam','A decommissioned standby left an inactive physical replication slot on the primary. What is the consequence?','The primary refuses new connections','Autovacuum begins running more frequently','Nothing, because slots expire automatically after 24 hours','The primary retains WAL indefinitely for that slot, which can fill pg_wal, and with hot_standby_feedback the slot can also hold back the vacuum horizon','D','A replication slot is a durable promise to keep WAL until the consumer has it, so an abandoned slot never releases that WAL. Slots must be dropped when the consumer is retired, or bounded with max_slot_wal_keep_size.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Performance Tuning','exam','Which command shows both the planner estimates and the real row counts, timings and buffer usage for a query?','EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)','EXPLAIN VERBOSE','EXPLAIN (COSTS OFF)','SELECT pg_stat_statements_reset()','A','ANALYZE executes the statement and reports actual rows and time per node, and BUFFERS adds the shared and local block counts. Plain EXPLAIN shows only the estimates, which is what hides a bad estimate from the reader.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Performance Tuning','exam','A DBA must identify which normalised statements consume the most total execution time across the cluster. What is the right tool?','The pg_stat_statements extension, ordered by total execution time','A single sample of pg_stat_activity','Setting log_min_duration_statement to zero permanently','pg_stat_user_tables','A','pg_stat_statements accumulates calls, total time and rows per normalised query, which is what finds the aggregate cost of a fast statement executed a million times. Sampling pg_stat_activity catches only what happens to be running at that instant.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Performance Tuning','hard','A query is served by an index, but EXPLAIN shows an Index Scan with a large number of heap fetches rather than an Index Only Scan. What explains it and what is the fix?','The index is bloated and needs to be rebuilt','work_mem is too small for the scan','The index type does not support index only scans and must be converted to a hash index','The visibility map is not current for that table, so vacuuming the table lets the planner use an index only scan, and adding the selected columns with INCLUDE makes the index covering','D','An index only scan still checks visibility unless the page is marked all visible in the visibility map, which vacuum maintains. If the query also selects columns absent from the index, the heap must be visited regardless until the index covers them.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Performance Tuning','exam','One reporting query spills a large sort to disk while the server has spare memory and few active sessions. What is the safest change?','Raise work_mem globally to 2 GB','Raise work_mem for that session or that role only, because the setting applies per sort or hash node and multiplies across nodes and concurrent sessions','Raise shared_buffers to 2 GB','Disable sorting by setting enable_sort to off','B','work_mem is allocated per operation, so a global increase can be multiplied by the number of sort and hash nodes in each plan and by the number of sessions. Scoping it to the reporting role gives the benefit without risking an out of memory event.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Performance Tuning','exam','A 2 TB table range partitioned on an event date is queried with a predicate on that date, yet every partition is scanned. What should be checked first?','That every partition carries identical indexes','That constraint_exclusion is enabled for declarative partitions','That the predicate is expressed on the partition key in a form the planner can prune with, since a non immutable expression, a wrapped function call on the column, or a type mismatch prevents partition pruning','That the parent table itself contains rows','C','Pruning requires the planner to compare the predicate against the partition bounds, which it cannot do when the column is wrapped in a function or compared across a type it must coerce at runtime. Declarative partitioning does not rely on constraint_exclusion.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Performance Tuning','exam','A large, busy table keeps bloating even though autovacuum is running. Which per table change is most appropriate?','Disable autovacuum for the table and vacuum it manually once a week','Lower autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor for that table so vacuum triggers after a smaller fraction of rows change, and raise the cost limit so it can keep up','Increase default_statistics_target','Run VACUUM FULL during peak hours','B','The default scale factor is a percentage of the table, so on a very large table vacuum starts only after an enormous number of dead rows accumulate. Per table storage parameters fix that without changing behaviour for small tables.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Performance Tuning','hard','A query filtering on two correlated columns, a city and its postcode, gets a row estimate thousands of times too low and chooses a nested loop. What is the correct remedy?','Increase random_page_cost','Create a partial index for each city value','Create extended statistics with CREATE STATISTICS on the functional dependency between the two columns and run ANALYZE','Set enable_nestloop to off in postgresql.conf','C','The planner multiplies per column selectivities and therefore underestimates correlated predicates badly; extended statistics teach it the dependency. Disabling a join type globally distorts every other plan on the server.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Replication','exam','What does a physical streaming standby replicate?','The WAL stream of the entire cluster byte for byte, so the standby is an exact physical copy and cannot replicate a subset of tables','Only the tables named in a publication','SQL statements that are re-executed on the standby','Only committed rows, in a logical row format','A','Physical replication ships WAL records describing block level changes for the whole cluster, which is why the standby is identical and read only. Selective, table level replication requires logical replication instead.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Replication','exam','A cluster has synchronous_commit on and synchronous_standby_names naming its single standby. That standby fails. What happens?','The primary carries on without waiting','The primary promotes itself','Reads begin to fail on the primary','Commits on the primary block until a synchronous standby is available, so a second candidate standby or a quorum setting is needed to keep writes flowing','D','Synchronous commit waits for the named standby to confirm the WAL, so losing the only candidate stalls every commit. Listing several candidates, or using the ANY quorum syntax, preserves durability without a single point of failure.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Replication','exam','Only three tables must be replicated to a reporting system running a different PostgreSQL major version. Which mechanism fits?','Physical streaming replication with hot standby','Scheduled pg_basebackup runs','Logical replication, with a publication for those three tables and a subscription on the target, which works across major versions','A foreign data wrapper with hourly materialized view refreshes','C','Logical replication decodes WAL into row changes for selected tables and applies them through normal SQL, so publisher and subscriber versions need not match. Physical replication requires an identical major version and replicates the entire cluster.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Replication','hard','A logical replication subscription is created and the initial copy succeeds, but sequences on the subscriber never advance and DDL changes on the publisher never appear. Why?','The subscription was created with copy_data set to false','The replication slot was dropped','wal_level was left at replica, so nothing would replicate at all','Logical replication propagates DML for the published tables only; it does not replicate DDL or sequence values, so both must be handled separately, typically at cutover','D','Logical decoding emits row changes for published tables and nothing else, which is why schema changes must be applied to both sides and sequences advanced manually before a cutover. If wal_level were wrong the initial copy would not have produced ongoing changes either.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Replication','exam','Long reporting queries on a hot standby are cancelled with a conflict with recovery error. Which trade off resolves it?','Set max_standby_streaming_delay to zero','Enable hot_standby_feedback so the primary delays cleaning up rows the standby still needs, accepting more bloat on the primary, or raise max_standby_streaming_delay to tolerate more replication lag','Disable WAL archiving','Promote the standby','B','The conflict occurs because the primary vacuumed rows a running standby query still needs, and the two available levers are telling the primary to wait or letting the standby fall further behind. Both cost something, which is why the choice is a trade off rather than a fix.'),
('EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional','Replication','exam','After a standby is promoted, the old primary must rejoin as a standby without taking a full base backup. Which tool is designed for this?','pg_rewind, provided the cluster was created with data checksums or wal_log_hints enabled','pg_resetwal','pg_upgrade','pg_receivewal','A','pg_rewind rewinds the diverged old primary to the point where the timelines split and copies only the changed blocks, which is far cheaper than a full base backup on a large cluster. It needs page hints, so either data checksums or wal_log_hints must have been enabled beforehand.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional');

do $proof$
declare
  r record;
  n bigint;
begin
  for r in
    select unnest(array[
      'AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Professional',
      'AWS Certified Developer - Associate',
      'TOGAF 9 Certified',
      'EDB PostgreSQL 12+ Professional'
    ]) as cert
  loop
    select count(*) into n from public.certification_exam_question where certification = r.cert;
    if n < 40 then
      raise exception 'certification bank % has only % questions, expected at least 40', r.cert, n;
    end if;
  end loop;
  raise notice 'cloud architecture certification banks proven: 4 certifications each with at least 40 questions';
end
$proof$;
